      "question": "What environmental challenges does Madagascar face?",
      "in_scope": true,
      "answer": "Madagascar faces several environmental challenges, including the need to adapt to and avert the consequences of climate change, which would require spending 15% of its GDP. The country has also lost more than 90% of its original forest due to human activities such as slash-and-burn agriculture, increased cattle herding, reliance on charcoal for cooking, and the cultivation of coffee as a cash crop. Additionally, Madagascar is experiencing issues related to undernourishment and hunger, with more than one million people in southern Madagascar struggling to get enough to eat, potentially leading to the country's first famine caused by climate change. The country is also water-stressed, with climate shocks and infrastructure gaps exacerbating the crisis. \n\nSources: [Climate] | Chunk #61, [Health] | Chunk #134, [Biodiversity and conservation] | Chunk #68, [Economy] | Chunk #95",
